      "editorData": "<div style=\"font-family:'Metropolis','Segoe UI',sans-serif;font-size:13px;color:#ffffff;padding:6px 12px\">\n<h2 style=\"font-size:15px;color:#ffffff;margin:4px 0 10px 0\">vSAN overview &mdash; which layer is broken?</h2>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\">The pager went off and you do not yet know where to look. Each table below is a <b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">layer of the IO path</b>, top to bottom, and the job of this screen is to point at one of them inside a minute. Then move to the matching detail screen.</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\"><b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">Normal is front end above back end.</b> The front end carries network hops, owner coordination and policy work the disks never see, so it should read higher. <b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">The two converging, or inverting, is the anomaly</b> &mdash; not either number on its own.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;color:#ffffff;margin:16px 0 6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #4a5a66;padding-bottom:3px\">Capacity</h3>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:600;color:#8fd3ff\">Free / Used / Total Capacity</span> and <span style=\"font-weight:600;color:#8fd3ff\">Space Efficiency Ratio</span> &mdash; here because exhaustion takes a cluster read-only, which is an outage rather than a performance problem.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;color:#ffffff;margin:16px 0 6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #4a5a66;padding-bottom:3px\">Front end &mdash; what VMs feel</h3>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\">DOM Client is where a guest's IO enters vSAN. <span style=\"font-weight:600;color:#8fd3ff\">Read / Write Latency</span> here is what a user means by \"the VM is slow\". <span style=\"font-weight:600;color:#8fd3ff\">Congestions</span> is vSAN deliberately slowing IO down to protect itself &mdash; any non-zero value is the cluster applying the brakes.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;color:#ffffff;margin:16px 0 6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #4a5a66;padding-bottom:3px\">Back end &mdash; the disks</h3>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\">DOM Component Manager is what the local devices actually did. Read it against the front end: <b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">front high and back low</b> means the problem is above the disks &mdash; network, owner, or policy. <b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">Both high</b> means the disks.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;color:#ffffff;margin:16px 0 6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #4a5a66;padding-bottom:3px\">Transport &mdash; RDT</h3>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\">vSAN's own protocol, above TCP. Can be slow with every NIC counter clean. <span style=\"font-weight:600;color:#8fd3ff\">Keepalive Reset</span> is cumulative since boot &mdash; watch whether it rises, not its value. The socket <span style=\"font-weight:600;color:#8fd3ff\">Min Outbound / Inbound Bytes</span> columns are free space, so <b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">low is bad</b>.</p>\n\n<h3 style=\"font-size:13px;color:#ffffff;margin:16px 0 6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #4a5a66;padding-bottom:3px\">Rebuild activity</h3>\n\n<p style=\"margin:5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#ffffff\">All zero means nothing is rebuilding, which is the healthy idle state. During a rebuild, read it beside the front end above: <b style=\"color:#ffffff;font-weight:700\">resync climbing while guest latency stays flat is healthy</b>; both climbing means the rebuild is eating production IO.</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin:14px 0 5px 0;line-height:1.45;color:#b6c2ca;font-style:italic\">Each table is one object &mdash; the cluster &mdash; so this screen costs the same whether you run four hosts or sixty-four.</p>\n</div>\n",
